      "writtenOn": "2023-06-29T23:06:13Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Actually, now that I look at the code again, I am wondering if my test to see if `cpu_init()` was called in RW was wrong. Originally, I was adding a debug print in `cpu_init()`, but it looks like the UART is enabled *after* `cpu_init()` is called, so that might have prevented the message from being printed.\n\nSkimming the jump logic, I don\u0027t see a reason why `cpu_init()` wouldn\u0027t be called. It looks like jumping is just doing a reset to the RW image, which should trigger all of the same initialization code and then jump to `main` (where `cpu_init()` is called).\n\nTry removing the call to `cpu_init_rw()`. **However**, you will also need to add logic to `cpu_init()` so that it clears the bit if it\u0027s already set:\n\n```\n\tif (!IS_ENABLED(CONFIG_UNALIGNED_ACCESS)) {\n\t\tCPU_NVIC_CCR |\u003d CPU_NVIC_CCR_UNALIGN_TRAP;\n\t} else {\n\t        CPU_NVIC_CCR \u0026\u003d ~CPU_NVIC_CCR_UNALIGN_TRAP;\n\t}\n```\n\n(and if that works, reverse the `if` statement so it\u0027s `if(IS_ENABLED` instead of `if(!IS_ENABLED` since that\u0027s more readable.)",
